# LUKSO MiniApp Next.js Template Rules

## Project Overview

This is a Next.js template for building mini-apps on the LUKSO blockchain using Universal Profiles (UPs). The template demonstrates wallet connection, profile search, and blockchain interactions through a donation interface.

## Project Structure

### Core Files
- [app/layout.tsx](mdc:app/layout.tsx) - Root layout with global styles
- [app/page.tsx](mdc:app/page.tsx) - Main application entry point with conditional rendering
- [package.json](mdc:package.json) - Dependencies and scripts

### Key Components
- [components/upProvider.tsx](mdc:components/upProvider.tsx) - **CRITICAL**: UP Provider context for wallet connection and blockchain state
- [components/Donate.tsx](mdc:components/Donate.tsx) - LYX token donation functionality
- [components/ProfileSearch.tsx](mdc:components/ProfileSearch.tsx) - Profile search using Envio GraphQL API
- [components/LuksoProfile.tsx](mdc:components/LuksoProfile.tsx) - Profile display using LUKSO web components

### Styling & Configuration
- [styles/globals.scss](mdc:styles/globals.scss) - Global styles with LUKSO web components
- [tailwind.config.ts](mdc:tailwind.config.ts) - Tailwind CSS configuration
- [tsconfig.json](mdc:tsconfig.json) - TypeScript configuration

## Architecture Patterns

### UP Provider Pattern
The [upProvider.tsx](mdc:components/upProvider.tsx) is the central hub for:
- UP Browser wallet connection
- Blockchain state management (chainId, accounts, contextAccounts)
- Viem client creation for transactions
- Event listeners for wallet state changes

### Component State Management
- Use `useUpProvider()` hook for accessing wallet state
- Components handle their own loading states
- Error handling is managed at component level

### Blockchain Integration
- Uses Viem for blockchain interactions
- ERC725.js for profile metadata fetching
- GraphQL queries via Envio for profile search

## Key Dependencies

### LUKSO Specific
- `@lukso/up-provider` - UP Browser wallet integration
- `@lukso/web-components` - Ready-made LUKSO UI components
- `@erc725/erc725.js` - Profile metadata fetching

### Blockchain & Web3
- `viem` - Ethereum library for transactions
- `graphql-request` - GraphQL client for Envio queries

### UI & Styling
- `tailwindcss` - Utility-first CSS framework
- `ethereum-blockies-base64` - Avatar generation fallback

## Development Guidelines

### Component Development
1. Always use TypeScript with proper type definitions
2. Import `useUpProvider` for wallet functionality
3. Handle loading and error states appropriately
4. Use LUKSO web components (`lukso-button`, `lukso-input`, etc.) for UI consistency

### Wallet Integration
- Check `walletConnected` state before blockchain operations
- Use `client` from UP Provider for transactions
- Handle both mainnet (chainId: 42) and testnet environments

### Profile Handling
- Use ERC725.js for fetching profile metadata
- Implement blockies fallback for profile images
- Handle IPFS URL conversion for profile images

### Styling Guidelines
- Use Tailwind classes for layout and spacing
- Leverage LUKSO web components for branded elements
- SCSS is available for complex styling needs

## Common Patterns

### Wallet Connection Check
```typescript
const { client, walletConnected, accounts } = useUpProvider();
if (!client || !walletConnected) return;
```

### Transaction Pattern
```typescript
const tx = await client.sendTransaction({
  account: accounts[0] as `0x${string}`,
  to: recipientAddress,
  value: parseUnits(amount.toString(), 18),
  chain: client.chain,
});
await waitForTransactionReceipt(client, { hash: tx });
```

### Profile Search Pattern
```typescript
const envioUrl = chainId === 42 ? ENVIO_MAINNET_URL : ENVIO_TESTNET_URL;
const data = await request(envioUrl, gqlQuery, { id: searchQuery });
```

## Environment Configuration

### Chain Configuration
- Mainnet: chainId 42, uses `lukso` chain from viem
- Testnet: uses `luksoTestnet` chain from viem
- RPC endpoints are configured in components

### IPFS Configuration
- Uses `https://api.universalprofile.cloud/ipfs/` as IPFS gateway
- Automatically converts `ipfs://` URLs to HTTP URLs

## Testing & Development

### Local Development
- Run `yarn dev` for development server
- Test with UP Browser extension
- Use localhost URL as mini-app in Grid

### Component Testing
- Each component should handle wallet connection state
- Test both connected and disconnected states
- Verify proper error handling

## Security Considerations

### Transaction Safety
- Always validate amounts before transactions
- Use proper error handling for failed transactions
- Implement loading states to prevent double submissions

### Address Validation
- Validate Ethereum addresses before use
- Handle invalid addresses gracefully
- Use proper typing for addresses (`0x${string}`)

## Performance Optimization

### Component Loading
- Lazy load web components only on client side
- Use proper loading states for async operations
- Implement proper cleanup for event listeners

### Image Optimization
- Use Next.js Image component for profile images
- Implement fallback images for failed loads
- Optimize IPFS image loading

## Deployment Considerations

### Build Process
- Use `yarn build` for production builds
- Pre-build step processes configuration
- Ensure all dependencies are properly installed

### Environment Setup
- Works with Vercel, Replit, and other cloud platforms
- Can be used as localhost mini-app in Grid
- No environment variables required for basic functionality
